INT. ROLLER RINK - DJ BOOTH - NIGHT

Disco ball turning, colored lights sliding across empty hardwood. The rink is closed; the booth is a cluttered box of turntables and cables.

JUNE (16, braids, rink-staff polo shirt) sits at the mixer wearing headphones around her neck. TEO (17, skinny, backpack on both shoulders) leans in the doorway, holding a paper bag.

TEO
You said ten o'clock.

JUNE
It's ten-oh-four.

TEO
I ran here.

JUNE
You walked. I watched you from the window.

Teo grins and hands her the bag. June pulls out a cassette tape in a cracked case.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Is this it?

TEO
My dad's mix. Nineteen eighty-four. He made it for my mom.

JUNE
And you want me to play it on the rink system.

TEO
Just once. Before they tear the place down.

June looks out at the empty floor, then back at the tape. A PHONE on the desk buzzes. She answers it, thumbing the speaker.

RAQUEL (V.O.)
(through the phone)
Junebug, where are you? Mom's been calling the house for an hour.

JUNE
Work, Raquel. I told you.

RAQUEL (V.O.)
It's a school night. Come home.

JUNE
Five more minutes.

RAQUEL (V.O.)
Who's there with you?

June clamps her hand over the phone. Teo shakes his head, mouthing, no. June hangs up.

JUNE (CONT'D)
She'll tell Mom.

TEO
Then play it fast.

June slides the tape into the deck. A fat bassline wobbles out, warped by age. They stand there, listening to a dead man's song fill the hall.

TEO (CONT'D)
That's him humming.

JUNE
That's the best part.

The booth light snaps on. MR. LINDQVIST (60s, bathrobe over his work clothes, a flashlight) fills the doorway.

LINDQVIST
Who authorized this?

JUNE
I did!

TEO
I did!

^JUNE
(talking over him)
It was my idea, sir!

^TEO
(talking over her)
She had nothing to do with it!

LINDQVIST
Turn it off.

June reaches for the stop button. Lindqvist's hand clamps on hers.

LINDQVIST (CONT'D)
No. Not off. Turn it up.

He sets his flashlight down and sits heavily on a stool. The bassline swells. Outside the booth, the disco ball keeps turning.
